PREFIX=/usr
MANDIR=/usr/share/man
DESTDIR=

#
#  Makefile for GXemul
#

BIN=gxemul
LIBS=$(XLIB) $(OTHERLIBS)

all: build

build: do_src
	$(CC) $(COPTIM) src/console/*.o src/cpus/*.o src/debugger/*.o src/devices/*.o src/disk/*.o src/file/*.o src/machines/*.o src/net/*.o src/core/*.o src/promemul/*.o src/symbol/*.o $(LIBS) -o $(BIN)

do_src:
	cd src; $(MAKE)

install:
	@echo Installing binaries, man page, and documentation...
	mkdir -p $(DESTDIR)$(PREFIX)/bin
	cp -f $(BIN) $(DESTDIR)$(PREFIX)/bin/
	mkdir -p $(DESTDIR)$(MANDIR)/man1
	cp -f man/gxemul.1 $(DESTDIR)$(MANDIR)/man1/
	mkdir -p $(DESTDIR)$(PREFIX)/share/doc/gxemul

codex
**Verdict: extend `#510` with a nonpositive-width guard. The original 468 UB pairs are correct; copying `#510` verbatim leaves 465 undefined, not 467.**

**Q1.** The three current mask initializers are:

[UBFX, line 1351](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:1351):

```c
uint32_t mask = (width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1);
```

[SBFX, line 1411](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:1411):

```c
uint32_t mask = (width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1);
```

[BFI, line 1460](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:1460):

```c
uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
```

Confirmed: two repaired siblings and one unrepaired site.

**Q2.** Their decoder-derived domains are:

| Handler | Decoder calculation | Handler width domain |
|---|---|---|
| [UBFX](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:4571) | `1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31)` | `1..32` |
| [SBFX](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:4578) | `1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31)` | `1..32` |
| [BFI](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:4564) | Independent `msb`, `lsb` in `0..31`; `width = msb - lsb + 1` | `-30..32` |

**Both extract handlers can produce 31.** Neither produces zero or negative widths. Their decoders also omit a `lsb + width <= 32` check; that does not introduce another undefined shift in these repaired handlers.

Under the 32-bit C integer model, `1U << 31` is defined, and the subtraction is unsigned. The guard handles 32; negative counts remain undefined. Thus verbatim transfer repairs **three pairs**: two at width 31 and one at width 32. These distinctions follow C’s shift rules, [§6.5.7 paragraphs 3–4](https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n1570.pdf).

My enumeration confirms:

- Original UB: `465 + 2 + 1 = 468`.
- Reversed ordering: `465 + 31 = 496`.
- Their intersection: **465**.
- Currently defined: `31 + 525 = 556`.

**Q3.** I re-extracted the [local ARM DDI 0100I PDF](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/_scratchpad/ddi0100i.pdf) and found no BFI/BFC occurrences. I cannot substantiate the proposed **UNPREDICTABLE** classification from that local manual.

Choose an empty field for `msb < lsb`, leaving the destination unchanged, and document this as an **emulator choice**. This also preserves the existing defined behavior at width zero.

**Q4.** Replace only BFI’s initializer:

```diff
-	uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
+	/*  Emulator choice: msb < lsb leaves the destination unchanged.  */
+	uint32_t mask = (width <= 0) ? 0U :
+	    (width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1);
```

Splitting the nonpositive domain makes preservation explicit:

| Width | Pairs | Original C behavior | Proposed behavior |
|---|---:|---|---|
| `< 0` | 465 | Undefined | Zero mask; destination unchanged |
| `0` | 31 | Defined | Same zero mask and unchanged destination |
| `1..30` | 525 | Defined | Identical mask and insertion |
| `31` | 2 | Undefined | Mask `0x7fffffffU`; 31-bit insertion |
| `32` | 1 | Undefined | Mask `0xffffffffU`; full-register copy, since `lsb == 0` |

The conditional evaluates the width shift only for `1..31`. The remaining shifts use `uint32_t` operands and `lsb` in `0..31`.

**No currently defined pair changes behavior.** For all 556 such pairs, the mask is numerically identical, so the unchanged destination-update statements produce identical results for every source and destination value. Enumeration also found zero mismatches against an independently constructed, bit-by-bit field mask across all 1024 pairs.

**Q5.** The useful runtime observable is a **UBSan diagnostic at the offending expression**, or failure status with recovery disabled—not a particular wrong register value. UBSan’s shift checks cover negative/excessive counts and signed-left-shift overflow in C. [UBSan documentation](https://clang.llvm.org/docs/UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.html)

UBSan is not literally the only honest detector: a sound static type/range analysis also establishes the defect. Ordinary result comparisons cannot establish definedness.

Your [CPU build Makefile](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/build-asan/src/cpus/Makefile:9) already specifies `-fsanitize=address,undefined`; this configuration includes UBSan. ASan alone would not detect this arithmetic UB. Also, **468 counts input pairs, not expected diagnostic lines**.

**Q6.** They remain two rounds. The decoder unconditionally supplies `&cpu->cd.arm.r[rm]`, including source field 15. This patch makes no operand-selection change and therefore leaves the reported BFC issue unresolved—including at the 525 ordinary, already-defined pairs.

The separation remains straightforward: this change repairs mask construction; `armbfcdecode` addresses source selection. Defined mask arithmetic also removes a confounder from subsequent BFC checks.

Validation here was mathematical enumeration and source review; no compiled sanitizer run was performed. **The patch is supplied but unapplied because this session’s filesystem is read-only.**
